---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const inventoryCode = `inventory = expressir.parse_population(open("model.p21").read())
# {"file_schemas": [...], "records": [{"id": 1, "keyword": "E", ...}],
#  "anchors": [["POINT_1", "#1"]], "references": [...]}

verdict = expressir.validate_population(["E", "POINT"], source)
# {"valid": true, "errors": []}`;
---

<Base title="Part 21">
  <h1>ISO 10303-21 populations</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    The exchange structure (header, anchor, reference, and data
    sections) is parsed by a compiled PARG grammar, not hand-rolled
    code: <code>assets/p21/iso-10303-21.parg</code> in
    <a href="https://github.com/lutaml/expressir-rs">expressir-rs</a>
    compiles to a checksummed artifact whose trees are pinned to the
    ruby grammar byte-for-byte on the ISO 10303-21 annex corpus.
  </p>

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <pre><code>{inventoryCode}</code></pre>

  <h2>The parity gate</h2>
  <p>
    The rust suite replays the ruby DSL grammar's trees on the annex
    corpus (annexes E, H, I, J) — a grammar change that drifts from
    the ruby behavior fails the build. The embedded accept/reject
    tests run at artifact compile time, so a grammar that cannot parse
    its own contract never seals.
  </p>
</Base>
